.TH executor 1 2025-03-28

.SH NAME

executor - executes assembly for Intel GPUs

.SH SYNOPSIS

executor [-d DEVICE] FILENAME [ARGS...]

executor [-d DEVICE] --oa OA [--oa-csv FILE] FILENAME [ARGS...]

executor [-d DEVICE] --oa list

executor -d list

.SH DESCRIPTION

Runs a Lua script that can perform data manipulation
and dispatch execution of compute shaders, written in the same
assembly format used by the gen assembler/parser or when dumping
shaders in debug mode.

The goal is to have a tool to experiment directly with certain
assembly instructions and the shared units without having to
instrument the drivers.

The program will pick the first available device unless -d is
passed with either the index or a substring of the device to use.
Use "-d list" to list available devices.

.SH PERFORMANCE COUNTERS

If --oa OA is passed, executor wraps every execute() call in an OA
performance query and writes one CSV row per execute().  The first row is
a header.  By default, CSV is printed to stdout after the script finishes.
With --oa-csv FILE, CSV is written to FILE as the script runs.

--oa OA selects the OA profile and optional counter filter.  OA has the
form PROFILE[:COUNTER1,COUNTER2].  If the counter list is omitted, all
counters in the selected profile are written.  If PROFILE is omitted, or
OA does not match a profile name, counters are selected from ComputeBasic.
--oa-csv only selects the output file and must be combined with --oa.
For example:

  --oa ComputeBasic
  --oa ComputeBasic:GpuTime,AvgGpuCoreFrequency
  --oa GpuTime,AvgGpuCoreFrequency

--oa list lists all OA profiles and counters and exits.  For example:

  executor --oa list

.SH SCRIPTING ENVIRONMENT

In addition to the regular Lua standard library the following variables and
functions are available

- alloc(SIZE_DWORDS|TABLE, opts?) -> mem
  Allocate from the implicit default context data buffer.  If the first
  argument is a table, infer the size from its largest integer key, zero
  the allocation, and fill it from the table.  opts may be a string name
  or a table containing name=STR (for use with @addr), fill=VALUE, and
  align=BYTES (a power-of-two GPU byte address alignment).  If no name
  is provided, allocations are named buf0, buf1, ... in creation order.

- surface_buffer(MEM, {format=STR}|STR) -> surface
  Create a buffer surface view over MEM and assign the next BTI.  Supported
  formats include r32uint, r32float, rgba32float, and rgba8unorm.

- surface_2d(MEM, {format=STR, width=INT, height=INT, stride=BYTES?}) -> surface
  Create a linear 2D surface view over MEM and assign the next BTI.

- sampler({filter=nearest|linear, min_filter=STR, mag_filter=STR, address=clamp|repeat|mirror, normalized=BOOL, nonnormalized=BOOL, min_lod=NUM, max_lod=NUM}?) -> sampler
  Create a sampler and assign the next sampler index.

- sampler_desc{op=STR, surface=SURFACE|INT, bti=SURFACE|INT, sampler=SAMPLER|INT, simd=8|16|32, mlen=INT, rlen=INT, header=BOOL, hp=BOOL} -> INT
  Build a sampler SEND descriptor for raw send.smpl assembly.

- execute(SRC)
- execute{src=SRC, thread_groups=N}
  Assemble and execute SRC.  thread_groups selects the number of 1D thread
  groups to dispatch, defaulting to 1.  The shader source can use @param
  directives; see the parameter list below.

- mem:fill(VALUE)
  Fill all dwords with a constant value.

- mem:set(TABLE, offset?)
  Write dwords from a sparse table (0-based indexing).

- mem:read(COUNT, offset?) -> TABLE
- mem:to_table() -> TABLE
  Read dwords into a Lua table.

- mem:dump(COUNT, offset?)
  Pretty print COUNT dwords starting at the given dword offset.

- surface:bti(), sampler:index()
  Return the implicitly assigned BTI or sampler index.

- mem:offset(), mem:addr(), mem:name()
  Return the dword offset relative to the data buffer, GPU byte address,
  or memory name.  Indexing (mem[IDX]) is 0-based, #mem returns the
  size in dwords.

- arg
  Table containing the command line arguments passed to the script, arg[0]
  is the script filename, followed by the ARGS.  Arguments consumed by
  executor itself are not in this table.

- dump(ARRAY|MEM, COUNT)
  Pretty print the first COUNT dwords from an array or memory object.

- devinfo
  Table containing device information:
    - ver, verx10: Gfx version and detailed Gfx version
    - has_dpas, has_bfloat16
    - max_slm_size: Maximum shared local memory reservation in bytes

.SH ASSEMBLY MACROS

In addition to regular instructions, the follow macros will generate
assembly code based on the Gfx version being executed.  Unlike in regular
instructions, register operands must be plain rN GRFs (no subregisters,
regions, or immediates).

- @eot
  Send an EOT message.

- @mov REG IMM
  Like a regular MOV but accepts numbers in both decimal and
  floating-point.

- @id REG
  Write a linear local invocation index into REG.  The index is
  hw_thread_id * SIMD_WIDTH + lane_id.

- @tg REG
  Write the linear thread-group id into REG.

- @globalid REG
  Write a linear global invocation index into REG.  The index is
  thread_group_id * hw_threads * SIMD_WIDTH + local_id.

- @addr DST_REG MEM [DWORD_INDEX]
  Move the GPU byte address of MEM into DST_REG.  MEM is a memory name.
  DWORD_INDEX may be an immediate or register dword index.

- @load DST_REG ADDR_REG
  Read dwords from the GPU byte address in ADDR_REG (a32).

- @store ADDR_REG SRC_REG
  Write dwords to the GPU byte address in ADDR_REG (a32).

- @barrier
  Synchronize multiple hardware threads within each thread group in the
  execution.  If shared local memory is reserved, also fence shared local
  memory.  Emits no code for a single hardware thread.

- @param hw_regs N
  Set the number of hardware register for execute(SRC).

- @param hw_threads N
  Set the number of hardware threads for execute(SRC).

- @param simd 8|16|32
  Set the SIMD width for execute(SRC).  Xe2+ accepts SIMD16/SIMD32;
  earlier platforms accept SIMD8/SIMD16/SIMD32.

- @param slm_size BYTES
  Reserve shared local memory for the execution.

- @param autoswsb
  Fill in simple software scoreboard annotations on Xe+ when missing,
  preserving annotations already present in the source.

- @syncnop
  Produce a coarse grained sync.nop (when applicable) to ensure data from
  macros above are read/written.

.SH GPU EXECUTION

Compute shaders are dispatched with SIMD8 before Xe2 and SIMD16 on Xe2+
by default.  Use @param simd WIDTH in the shader to select another
supported SIMD width.  Xe2+ supports SIMD16 and SIMD32; earlier platforms
support SIMD8/SIMD16/SIMD32.  By default one hardware thread is dispatched.
Use @param hw_threads N in the shader to execute N hardware threads per
thread group.  Use thread_groups=N in the execute{} table to dispatch N
linear thread groups.  Use @param slm_size BYTES in the shader to reserve
shared local memory for the execution.
Context allocations are sub-allocated from a data buffer bound at graphics
address 0x30000000.  @addr produces a32 byte addresses for @load/@store
messages.

The executor reserves r0..r1 for its own payload and the last 4 GRFs
(as specified by @param hw_regs) for macros. Macro operands are
checked for overlap, but raw assembly instructions are not.

The number of registers available can be set with @param hw_regs N. By
default 128 registers are available.

The Gfx versions have differences in their assembly and shared units, so
other than very simple examples, scripts for this program will be either
specific to a version or provide shader variants for multiple versions.

.SH ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES

The following INTEL_DEBUG values (comma separated) are used:

 - bat             Dumps the batch buffer.
 - color           Uses colors for the batch buffer dump.
 - cs              Dumps the source after macro processing
                   the final assembly.

.SH EXAMPLE

The script

  local buf = alloc({ [42] = 0x100 })  -- gets default name buf0

  execute [[
    @param autoswsb

    @addr    r2      buf0 42
    @load    r3      r2

    @id      r4
    @addr    r5      buf0 r4

    add (8)  r6      r3      r4

    @store   r5      r6
    @eot
  ]]

  dump(buf, 4)

Will produce the output

   [0x00000000] 0x00000100 0x00000101 0x00000102 0x00000103

More examples can be found in the examples/ directory in the source code.

